Let $n\ge 2$ and consider holomorphic self-maps of $\mathbb{C}^n$. A continuous self-map $T$ of a topological space $X$ is called universal (or hypercyclic) if there exists $x\in X$ whose forward orbit $\{T^k(x):k\ge 0\}$ is dense in $X$. One says that $T$ admits a non-trivial invariant Borel probability measure $\mu$ if $\mu(T^{-1}E)=\mu(E)$ for every Borel set $E$ and $\mu$ is not concentrated on a single periodic orbit.

For generic holomorphic self-maps of $\mathbb{C}^n$ (in suitable Baire-category and topological senses), one knows that universality (existence of dense orbits) and the existence of non-trivial invariant Borel probability measures are typical phenomena.

Investigate to what extent the analogous statements hold within the following more restrictive classes of dynamical systems on $\mathbb{C}^n$:

(a) the group $\mathrm{Aut}(\mathbb{C}^n)$ of biholomorphic automorphisms of $\mathbb{C}^n$;

(b) the subgroup $\mathrm{Aut}_1(\mathbb{C}^n)$ of volume-preserving holomorphic automorphisms of $\mathbb{C}^n$ (those preserving the standard holomorphic volume form $dz_1\wedge\cdots\wedge dz_n$);

(c) for $n$ even, the subgroup $\mathrm{Aut}_\omega(\mathbb{C}^n)$ of holomorphic automorphisms preserving the standard holomorphic symplectic form $\omega=\sum_{j=1}^{n/2} dz_{2j-1}\wedge dz_{2j}$.

Specifically, determine whether a generic (in an appropriate Baire-category sense within each of the above classes equipped with the topology of locally uniform convergence) automorphism $F$ of $\mathbb{C}^n$ in each of these classes:

(i) is universal, i.e. possesses a dense orbit in $\mathbb{C}^n$;

(ii) admits a non-trivial $F$-invariant Borel probability measure on $\mathbb{C}^n$.

